# G095 — Excel Spreadsheet Exporter

*v2. See `excel-exporter.md` for prose: sparse (row, col)-addressed
cells, lazy formula evaluation, per-evaluation visit-set for cycle
detection, range expansion as notation sugar, CSV as interop export.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Ledger View

[vault-ledger-view @year
    IN ORDER TO
        populate a grid from ledger transactions
        with a SUM formula that tracks edits automatically
        and export the evaluated grid as CSV on demand

    @sheet <- [xl[new]]
    @txns  <- [vault[read-ledger {year: @year}]]

    [xl[set-text {sheet: @sheet, addr: "A1", s: "amount (cents)"}]]
    [xl[set-text {sheet: @sheet, addr: "B1", s: "category"}]]
    [xl[set-text {sheet: @sheet, addr: "D1", s: "Total:"}]]

    for (@i, @t) in @txns enumerated
        [xl[set-number {sheet: @sheet,
                         addr: "A" + (@i + 2),
                         n: @t:amount-cents}]]
        [xl[set-text   {sheet: @sheet,
                         addr: "B" + (@i + 2),
                         s: @t:category}]]

    [xl[set-formula
         {sheet: @sheet, addr: "E1",
          f: "=SUM(A2:A" + (@txns count + 1) + ")"}]]

    [ui[render-grid {sheet: @sheet}]]

    sync @user-events {
        export ->
            [vault[save
                    {path: "exports/ledger-" + @year + ".csv",
                     content: [xl[to-csv {sheet: @sheet}]]}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[xl
    new
    set-number        {sheet, addr, n}
    set-text          {sheet, addr, s}
    set-formula       {sheet, addr, f}
    evaluate          {sheet, addr}                   ;; || cycle | bad-ref
    to-csv            {sheet}
    parse-address     {addr}
    format-address    {row, col}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: lazy evaluation `where` formula is stored

[xl[evaluate {sheet: @s, addr: @a}]
    IN ORDER TO
        return the current value of @a re-computing every formula
        so that downstream cells reflect edits automatically

    @cell <- @s:cells at @a
        where kind is BLANK    -> empty value
        where kind is NUMBER   -> @cell:number
        where kind is TEXT     -> @cell:text
        where kind is FORMULA  -> evaluate @cell:formula recursively
            where @a is not already in the current visit-set
                -> push @a, recurse, pop @a on return
            otherwise
                -> #cycle detected at @a
]

The cell stores the formula, not the result. State is minimal: only
leaf values are stored; derived values recompute every read. Same
shape every reactive framework (React, signals, spreadsheets) uses.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: range as `where`-all address unroll

[xl[evaluate {sheet: @s, addr: "SUM(A1:A5)"}]
    IN ORDER TO
        treat A1:A5 as notation for five independent cell evaluations
        so a new function (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, COUNT) costs nothing

    cells at (r, c) where r in [0, 4] and c = 0
        each evaluated through the usual path
        then reduced by the calling function
]

Ranges do not introduce a new primitive. They unroll into the cell-
evaluation mechanism that already exists — same way SQL's `IN`
unrolls into `=` comparisons.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @s <- [xl[new]]
    [xl[set-number  {sheet: @s, addr: "A1", n: 10}]]
    [xl[set-number  {sheet: @s, addr: "A2", n: 20}]]
    [xl[set-number  {sheet: @s, addr: "A3", n: 30}]]
    [xl[set-formula {sheet: @s, addr: "B1", f: "=SUM(A1:A3)"}]]
    [xl[set-formula {sheet: @s, addr: "B2", f: "=AVG(A1:A3)"}]]
    [xl[set-formula {sheet: @s, addr: "B3", f: "=B1 * 2"}]]

    -> [xl[evaluate {sheet: @s, addr: "B1"}]]   ;; -> 60
    -> [xl[evaluate {sheet: @s, addr: "B2"}]]   ;; -> 20
    -> [xl[evaluate {sheet: @s, addr: "B3"}]]   ;; -> 120

    -> [xl[to-csv {sheet: @s}]]
    ;; -> "10,60\n20,20\n30,120\n"
]
